GitHub Workflow (Beginner-Friendly)

A. Create the Repository

1. Sign in to [https://github.com](https://github.com?utm_source=chatgpt.com).
2. Select New Repository.
3. Enter the repository name: `plsql-assignment4-<studentID1>-<studentID2>` (replace both placeholders with the students' IDs).
4. Choose Public as required by the assignment.
5. Add a README file only if you are not uploading an existing project README. Avoid creating duplicate files.

B. Upload Files Using the GitHub Website

1. Open your newly created repository.
2. Select Add file → Upload files.
3. Upload all project files and folders while maintaining the correct directory structure.
4. Enter a clear commit message, for example: `Add initial schema and PL/SQL implementation`.
5. Select Commit changes.

C. Upload the Project Using Git (Recommended)

Install Git, open a terminal in your project folder, and execute the following commands:

```
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Add student registration PL/SQL project"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/plsql-assignment4-STUDENTID1-STUDENTID2.git
git push -u origin main
```

Replace the remote URL with the exact URL of your GitHub repository.

If Git requests authentication, use GitHub's supported sign-in method or a personal access token. Never include your password or access token in the repository.

D. Group Collaboration (Two Members)

Both students should contribute to the project and make commits using their individual GitHub accounts.

Examples of meaningful commit messages:

- Create relational schema and sample data.
- Implement registration and grade procedures.
- Add functions and test scripts.
- Document performance and presentation notes.

Each member should make genuine contributions. Do not create artificial commits or claim work that was completed by someone else.

E. Final Submission Checklist

- The repository is public and has the required name.
- Both members' full names and student IDs are included in the README file.
- All SQL scripts and documentation are uploaded.
- All scripts compile successfully, and tests have been performed in Oracle.
- Actual project screenshots are included in the screenshots/ folder.
- Both members have made meaningful commits.
- AI usage is accurately disclosed in the README file.
- The repository URL is submitted through the assignment Google Form before the class deadline.
